%% kafka sequence diagram
sequenceDiagram
    title Smeagol Engine Kafka Flow
    autonumber
    participant FE as smeagol-engine-frontend
    participant SE as smeagol-engine-backend
    participant MQ as Kafka cluster
    participant CE as calculation-engine
    participant SRC as source database
    participant OBJ as object storage
    participant SDB as smeagol persistence DB

    FE->>SE: POST /jobs (systemId, requestId, params)
    SE->>SE: Idempotency check (systemId, requestId)
    SE->>MQ: Produce JOB_REQUEST(jobId, systemId, params)
    MQ-->>CE: Consume JOB_REQUEST (consumer group calc-engine)
    CE-->>SE: ACK via JOB_ACCEPTED(jobId) topic
    SE-->>FE: 202 Accepted(jobId)

    CE->>SRC: Query via cursor/stream
    SRC-->>CE: Rows stream (1,000,000 x 50)
    CE->>OBJ: Write chunked Parquet + manifest
    CE->>MQ: Produce JOB_COMPLETED(eventId, jobId, resultUris, checksums)

    MQ-->>SE: Consume JOB_COMPLETED
    SE->>OBJ: Read manifest + files
    SE->>SDB: Bulk load to staging
    SE->>SDB: Merge/upsert transaction
    SE->>SE: Mark job COMPLETED

    FE->>SE: GET /jobs/{jobId}
    SE-->>FE: status=COMPLETED, rowCount=1000000

%% Kafka takeaways vs gRPC
%% - Kafka decouples SE and CE with replayable topics, smoothing bursts and enabling failover at the cost of higher end-to-end latency.
%% - Message contracts need explicit versioning and schema governance (e.g., Schema Registry) whereas gRPC enforces proto contracts at compile time.
%% - Back-pressure handled through consumer lag/partitions rather than synchronous flow control, so operational tooling (lag monitors, retention settings) becomes critical.
